{"name":"Foxy Lady","hex":"#d5a6ad","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"foxy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The recreational drug 5-methoxy-dimethyltryptamine (5-MeO-DMT)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the qualities of a fox.","Cunning, sly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/foxy"},{"term":"lady","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The mistress of a household.","A woman of breeding or higher class, a woman of authority."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To address as “lady”."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lady"}],"entries":[{"title":"Foxy Lady","blocks":[{"text":"\"Foxy Lady\" is a song by the Jimi Hendrix Experience. It first appeared on their 1967 debut album Are You Experienced and was later issued as their third single in the U.S. with the alternate spelling. It is one of Hendrix's best-known songs and was frequently performed in concerts throughout his career. Rolling Stone magazine placed the song at number 153 on its list of the \"500 Greatest Songs of All Time\"."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/3d/Foxy_Lady_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foxy_Lady"},{"title":"Foxy Lady (Cher album)","blocks":[{"text":"Foxy Lady is the eighth studio album by American singer-actress Cher, released on July 10, 1972 by Kapp Records. Following the commercial success of the previous album Gypsies, Tramps & Thieves, Cher again collaborated with Snuff Garrett (producer), Al Capp (arrangements) and her then-husband Sonny Bono (co-producer). \nFoxy Lady was the second and last record for Kapp. The album was promoted on Cher's successful The Sonny & Cher Comedy Hour show. \nIt was well received by critics, but unlike her previous effort Gypsies, Tramps & Thieves, had only moderate chart and sales success."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/b6/Jjjio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foxy_Lady_(Cher_album)"},{"title":"Lady","blocks":[{"text":"A lady is a woman who behaves in a polite way. Once used to describe only women of a high social class or status, the female counterpart of lord, now it may refer to any adult woman, as gentleman can be used for men."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lady"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/John_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Lady_of_Shalott_-_Google_Art_Project_edit.jpg/500px-John_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Lady_of_Shalott_-_Google_Art_Project_edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/John_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Lady_of_Shalott_-_Google_Art_Project_edit.jpg/1280px-John_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Lady_of_Shalott_-_Google_Art_Project_edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/John_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Lady_of_Shalott_-_Google_Art_Project_edit.jpg/500px-John_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Lady_of_Shalott_-_Google_Art_Project_edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"John William Waterhouse's The Lady of Shalott, 1888 (Tate Gallery, London, England)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJohn_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Lady_of_Shalott_-_Google_Art_Project_edit.jpg"}],"sounds":{"foxy":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bc/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-foxy.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/b/bc/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-foxy.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-foxy.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.or